</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Ablation study</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Individual effects of memory reweighting and SAM.    </span>
We toggle off memory reweighting or SAM in <em id="A2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ReMem</em> to explore their individual effects. As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ B.1 Ablation study ‣ Appendix B Additional experiment results ‣ ReMem: Mutual Information-Aware Fine-tuning of Pretrained Vision Transformers for Effective Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, memory reweighting and SAM can both significantly boost the student performance, while the best performance can be achieved when the two are combined.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T10" class="ltx_table">
